    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where a freelance developer is the right call</h2>
    <p>A freelancer is the better choice when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You have technical skill in house.</strong> If someone on your staff can read code or fix a style problem, you can own the code and run the site yourself for years.</li>
      <li><strong>You need a one-time project.</strong> A landing page, a campaign site, or a one-off tool suits a freelancer who finishes and moves on.</li>
      <li><strong>You need one small feature.</strong> Freelancers excel at tightly scoped work, such as adding a calculator or fixing a specific bug.</li>
      <li><strong>Your budget is tight.</strong> If a freelancer is all you can afford and you accept the risk, go ahead, and plan for year two.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where a freelance hire costs you more</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Each new freelancer starts over.</strong> Freelancers move on to other contracts, full-time jobs, or the next project. The one after them bills hourly to learn the first one's code, if they'll take it on at all.</li>
      <li><strong>Uneven quality.</strong> Marketplace freelancers range from excellent to resellers of stock WordPress themes, and telling them apart takes the skill you're hiring for.</li>
      <li><strong>Local search comes second.</strong> Search markup (schema), a match with your Google Business Profile, and a page for each area take technical skill and service-business knowledge. Few freelancers have both.</li>
      <li><strong>You run the hosting.</strong> You set up hosting, the security certificate, domain settings, backups, and monitoring, or pay the freelancer hourly to do it.</li>
      <li><strong>No agreement when it breaks.</strong> A freelancer may say they can't help or no longer work on that code, so you pay someone else by the hour to fix it.</li>
    </ul>

